<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neman</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nemunas, Niemen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neman, or Nemunas, is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> about 900 kilometres long, rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> to the Curonian Lagoon on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Its lower course forms the border between Lithuania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the frontier and battleground between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and in 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> met on a raft on the river at Tilsit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neman.jpg" alt="Neman" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neman_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
